#### How many sending addresses the target rate needs

{% set per_ip = delivered / drain_span / addrs %}
{# The receiver's own allowance per address, per second, from the volume cap. The unit is a
   Postfix time value: a bare number means seconds, s/m/h are explicit. #}
{% set unit_raw = postfix_sink_anvil_rate_time_unit | default('60s') | string %}
{% set unit_n = unit_raw | regex_replace('[^0-9]', '') | int %}
{% set unit_s = unit_n * (3600 if unit_raw.endswith('h') else (60 if unit_raw.endswith('m') else 1)) %}
{% set cap_per_ip = ((postfix_sink_client_message_rate_limit | int) / unit_s) if unit_s > 0 else 0 %}
{% set saturated = cap_per_ip > 0 and per_ip >= (cap_per_ip * 0.8) %}
{# Two different failures look alike in the per-address rate and need opposite advice, so they
   are told apart by what the sender was ATTEMPTING. Below the allowance it was simply too
   slow. Above it, it was pushing harder than permitted and still not using its quota, which
   is a pacing problem and not a capacity one. #}
{% set attempts_per_ip = (attempts / drain_span / addrs) if (drain_span > 0 and addrs > 0) else 0 %}
{% set outpacing = attempts_per_ip >= cap_per_ip %}
On this run {{ addrs }} address{{ 'es' if addrs > 1 else '' }} delivered
{{ (delivered / drain_span) | round(1) }} recipients/s in total as counted by the receiver,
i.e. **{{ per_ip | round(2) }} recipients/s per address**, against an allowance of
{{ cap_per_ip | round(2) }}/s per address.

{% if saturated %}
The addresses were at their quota, so throughput here is bounded by the provider rather than
by Postal, and it scales with the number of addresses rather than with cores. Reaching the
target of {{ bench_target_rate }} recipients/s therefore needs
**about {{ ((bench_target_rate | float) / per_ip) | round(0, 'ceil') | int }} sending
addresses**.

This figure is only as good as the limits it was measured against, and those limits are an
assumption: nobody has supplied the destination-domain mix or the throttling actually
observed in production. Read it as the shape of the answer — that capacity is bought in
addresses, not in cores — and not as a procurement number. It also assumes every destination
throttles alike, whereas a real mix has a few large providers with hard limits and a long
tail with looser ones.
{% else %}
{% if outpacing %}
> **No address count is derived from this run, and the reason is worth more than the number
> would have been.** Each address delivered {{ per_ip | round(2) }} recipients/s against an
> allowance of {{ cap_per_ip | round(2) }}/s —
> {{ ((per_ip / cap_per_ip) * 100) | round(0) | int if cap_per_ip > 0 else 0 }} % of what it
> was permitted — while ATTEMPTING {{ attempts_per_ip | round(1) }}/s per address, that is
> {{ (attempts_per_ip / cap_per_ip) | round(1) if cap_per_ip > 0 else 0 }} times the
> allowance. The sender was not too slow. It could not use the quota it had.

That is a mismatch between two clocks, and it is the most transferable finding this run
produces. The receiver meters volume over {{ unit_raw }}; Postal defers a refused message by
five minutes and cannot shorten that — the base period is hardcoded. So Postal empties its
quota for the window in a burst, is refused for the rest of it, and every refused recipient
leaves the ready pool for five minutes instead of the seconds it would take for the quota to
roll over. The next window opens with the quota unused and much of the work parked.

The consequence for our own build is concrete: **pacing output to the receiver's rate is worth
more than raising delivery concurrency.** Concurrency here was already several times the
allowance and bought nothing. A sender that spread the same volume evenly would approach
{{ cap_per_ip | round(2) }}/s per address without a single refusal, and the address count for
the target rate would follow from that instead of from this run.
{% else %}
> **No address count is derived from this run: the sender, not the receiver, was the limit.**
> Each address delivered {{ per_ip | round(2) }} recipients/s against an allowance of
> {{ cap_per_ip | round(2) }}/s while attempting only {{ attempts_per_ip | round(1) }}/s.
> Dividing the achieved rate by the address count and extrapolating would answer "how many
> addresses would this Postal need at this speed", which reads as the provider's limit and is
> not it. Raise the delivery concurrency until the per-address ATTEMPT rate at least reaches
> the allowance, then the division means what it appears to.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% elif bench_sink_throttled | default(false) | bool and throttled == 0 %}
The number of addresses needed for the target rate is not derived here: it is only meaningful
once the receiver actually refused something. Without refusals the rate reflects Postal's own
ceiling, and dividing it by the address count would produce a figure that means the opposite
of what it appears to.
{% elif bench_sink_throttled | default(false) | bool and addrs == 0 %}
The number of addresses needed for the target rate is not derived here: the load was spread
