## What this run does not prove

{% if (postfix_sink_response_delay_ms | default(0) | int) > 0 %}
- The sink answers with an artificial delay of {{ postfix_sink_response_delay_ms }} ms per
  response, which models the network latency of a real MX.
{% else %}
- The sink accepts and discards almost instantly, while a real MX holds a connection for
  hundreds of milliseconds. Set `postfix_sink_response_delay_ms` to measure closer to
  production — without it draining was overstated sevenfold once already.
{% endif %}
{% if bench_sink_throttled | default(false) | bool %}
- The receiver's limits are an ASSUMPTION. Nobody has supplied the destination-domain mix or
  the throttling actually observed in production, so `bench_sink_profiles.{{ bench_sink_profile }}`
  holds typical values for a large MX. Every number derived from them — above all the count of
  sending addresses — inherits that status.
- All destinations throttle identically here. A real mix is a few large providers with hard
  limits and a long tail with looser ones, and the aggregate ceiling of such a mix is not the
  ceiling measured against one uniform limit.
- Greylisting and reputation-based daily quotas are not modelled at all, and neither are
  permanent rejections: the only failures in this run are the ones the caps produced.
{% else %}
- The sink imposes no per-IP limits: unlimited concurrent sessions and unlimited volume from
  a single address, which no recipient provider allows. Retries, deferred delivery and
  suppression are therefore not exercised — the profile contains 100 % successful responses.
  Set `bench_sink_profile=provider` to measure against a receiver that refuses.
- In particular, the effective delivery concurrency reached here is not attainable from one
  address in production, and any conclusion about IP pools drawn from this run describes a
  receiver that does not throttle.
{% endif %}
{% if bench_report_kind == 'drain' %}
- The queue was filled with {{ bench_drain_queue_total_at_start }} rows, and the result
  applies to exactly that length. Transferring it to another length is invalid: both
  hot worker queries are not covered by indexes, so the cost of processing
  a message depends on how many rows sit in the table.
{% else %}
- The queue started empty, and the cost of processing a message grows with its length.
  Neither of the two hot worker queries is covered by an index: `queued_messages`
  has three — `domain`, `message_id`, `server_id`. The claim query
  stops at the first suitable row, so with a fully ready
  queue it is cheap; batch assembly, however (`batchable_messages`, up to 100 rows
  with the same `batch_key`), stops only once a hundred are collected or at the end of the
  table, and with {{ bench_profiles[bench_profile].destination_domains }} domains
  a hundred is almost never reached — that is, EVERY message costs
  a scan of the whole queue. Queue length is measured by `playbooks/drain.yml`.
{% endif %}
- The domain distribution is quadratic rather than Zipf: the share of the hottest
  domain is lower than it would be with a true Zipf, which means Postal's batching
  is exercised less.
- The queue sampler runs a `COUNT(*)` over `queued_messages` every
  {{ bench_sample_interval_s }} s on the same MariaDB as the system under test — that is
  the measuring apparatus's own load.
- The run lasted {{ window }} s. Extending it to a full day is extrapolation: the cost of
  processing a message grows with the accumulated volume.
{% if bench_run_class == 'debug' %}
- The components shared hardware, so these numbers must not be compared with a build
  measured differently.
{% endif %}
